    {
        title: "Module 1: WHS Legislation, Duty of Care & Risk Management",
        durationMinutes: 45,
        sequenceOrder: 1,
        contentType: 'TEXT',
        contentUrl: null,
        content: "WHS LEGISLATION, DUTY OF CARE & RISK MANAGEMENT (CPCWHS1001)\n\n1. Harmonised WHS Legislative Framework\n- The Work Health and Safety (WHS) Act 2011 and State WHS Regulations set the legal standard for workplace health and safety across Australia.\n- Objective: To secure the health and safety of workers and workplaces by eliminating or minimizing risks arising from work.\n\n2. Primary Duty of Care - The PCBU\n- Under the WHS Act, the Person Conducting a Business or Undertaking (PCBU / Employer) holds the PRIMARY DUTY OF CARE.\n- PCBUs must ensure, so far as is reasonably practicable:\n  * A safe work environment without risks to health and safety.\n  * Safe plant, machinery, structures, and safe systems of work.\n  * Safe use, handling, and storage of plant, structures, and substances.\n  * Provision of adequate facilities for worker welfare.\n  * Provision of necessary information, training, instruction, and supervision.\n  * Provision of mandatory personal protective equipment (PPE) at NO COST to workers.\n\n3. Worker Obligations & Duty of Care\n- Workers must:\n  * Take reasonable care for their own personal health and safety.\n  * Take reasonable care that their actions or omissions do not adversely affect others.\n  * Comply with any reasonable instruction given by the PCBU to comply with WHS laws.\n  * Cooperate with reasonable workplace safety policies and procedures.\n\n4. Right to Refuse or Stop Unsafe Work\n- A worker has the absolute legal right to cease or refuse to carry out work if they have a reasonable concern that the work would expose them or others to a serious risk to health and safety from an immediate or imminent hazard.\n- The worker must inform the PCBU as soon as practicable and remain available to carry out suitable alternative safe work.\n\n5. High-Risk Construction Work (HRCW) & Safe Work Method Statements (SWMS)\n- High-Risk Construction Work includes 18 specific high-hazard activities defined by WHS Regulations:\n  * Risk of a person falling more than 2 metres.\n  * Work on or near energised electrical installations or services.\n  * Work in or near a confined space or shaft/trench deeper than 1.5 metres.\n  * Demolition of an element of a structure that is load-bearing.\n  * Involving tilt-up or precast concrete.\n  * Work on or near pressurized gas distribution mains or piping.\n  * Work carried out in an area with movement of powered mobile plant.\n- Mandatory SWMS: A Safe Work Method Statement (SWMS) MUST be prepared before any high-risk construction work begins. The SWMS must identify the hazards, assess the risks, describe the control measures, and be signed off by all workers involved.\n\n6. Hazard vs Risk & Hierarchy of Controls\n- Hazard: Anything with potential to cause harm, injury, illness, or damage.\n- Hierarchy of Risk Control: 1. Elimination (most effective), 2. Substitution, 3. Isolation, 4. Engineering Controls, 5. Administrative Controls, 6. PPE (least effective, last line of defense)."
    },
    {
        title: "Module 2: Site Safety Signs, PPE & Incident Reporting",
        durationMinutes: 45,
        sequenceOrder: 2,
        contentType: 'TEXT',
        contentUrl: null,
        content: "SITE SAFETY SIGNS, PPE REQUIREMENTS & INCIDENT REPORTING (AS 1319)\n\n1. Australian Standard AS 1319 Safety Sign Categories\nUnderstanding site safety signs is a core legal competency on construction sites:\n- Danger Signs: Indicate life-threatening conditions or immediate hazards.\n  * Appearance: White background with a RED OVAL inside a BLACK RECTANGULAR header at the top, containing the word 'DANGER' in bold white text (e.g. Danger High Voltage, Danger Deep Excavation).\n- Warning Signs: Warn of conditions that are hazardous but not life-threatening.\n  * Appearance: YELLOW EQUILATERAL TRIANGLE with a black border and black symbol/text (e.g. Slippery Surface, Flammable Gas).\n- Mandatory Signs: Specify an instruction or action that MUST be carried out.\n  * Appearance: BLUE CIRCLE with a white symbol/pictogram on a white background (e.g. Eye Protection Must Be Worn, Hard Hat Required).\n- Prohibition Signs: Specify actions that are STRICTLY FORBIDDEN.\n  * Appearance: RED CIRCULAR RING with a 45-degree diagonal slash over a black symbol (e.g. No Smoking, No Unauthorized Entry).\n- Emergency Information Signs: Indicate location of emergency facilities.\n  * Appearance: GREEN RECTANGLE with white text or symbols (e.g. First Aid Kit, Emergency Assembly Area, Emergency Exit).\n- Fire Signs: Indicate location of fire alarms and firefighting equipment.\n  * Appearance: RED RECTANGLE with white text or symbols (e.g. Fire Extinguisher, Fire Hose Reel).\n\n2. Mandatory Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)\n- The PCBU is legally responsible for providing workers with required PPE.\n- Four Core Mandatory PPE Items on Australian Construction Sites:\n  1. Safety Helmet (Hard Hat) compliant with AS/NZS 1801.\n  2. High-Visibility Day/Night Garment compliant with AS/NZS 4602.1.\n  3. Steel-Cap Safety Boots compliant with AS/NZS 2210.3.\n  4. Eye Protection (Safety Glasses) compliant with AS/NZS 1337.1.\n- PPE Maintenance: Workers must inspect PPE before each use. If PPE is damaged, cracked, torn, or expired, it must be IMMEDIATELY tagged out of service, reported to the supervisor, and replaced before work starts.\n\n3. Workplace Incident & Injury Reporting\n- Importance: All workplace injuries, incidents, and near misses must be reported immediately, regardless of how minor they appear. Reporting ensures the worker receives medical treatment, prevents injuries from worsening, and enables the PCBU to investigate and correct the hazard to protect coworkers.\n- Notifiable Incidents: Under the WHS Act, the regulator (SafeWork / WorkSafe) must be notified immediately if an incident results in death, serious illness/injury (requiring immediate hospital admission or treatment within 48 hours), or is a dangerous incident (e.g. crane collapse, scaffolding collapse, uncontrolled gas leak). The incident site must not be disturbed until an inspector arrives."
    },
    {
        title: "Module 3: Emergency Procedures & Site Induction Requirements",
        durationMinutes: 45,
        sequenceOrder: 3,
        contentType: 'TEXT',
        contentUrl: null,
        content: "EMERGENCY PROCEDURES, SITE INDUCTIONS & MOBILE PLANT SAFETY\n\n1. Emergency Response Protocols\n- In the event of a fire, uncontrolled gas release, chemical spill, or structural collapse:\n  * Raise the alarm immediately (shout, break-glass alarm, notify supervisor, call emergency radio).\n  * Ensure personal safety; do not place yourself in danger to fight fires or retrieve possessions.\n  * Evacuate immediately via designated emergency egress routes.\n  * Proceed directly to the designated Emergency Assembly Point / Muster Area.\n  * Report to the Area Fire Warden for roll-call and remain at the assembly point until the official 'All Clear' is given.\n\n2. Construction Induction Types\n- General Construction Induction (White Card / CPCWHS1001): Nationally recognized card verifying baseline construction safety awareness. It does not expire as long as you remain active in the industry (in WA/QLD, valid indefinitely unless out of industry for 2+ consecutive years).\n- Site-Specific Induction: Conducted upon arrival at each new site. Covers site layout, local hazards, emergency alarms, evacuation routes, muster points, first aid facilities, and site-specific policies.\n- Task-Specific Induction: Reviews SWMS, job steps, and equipment requirements prior to starting specific construction tasks.\n\n3. Working Around Powered Mobile Plant\n- Mobile plant (excavators, dump trucks, skid steers, forklifts) is a leading cause of fatalities on construction sites.\n- Blind Spots: Areas around a vehicle or machine where the seated operator cannot see pedestrians or objects directly through windows or mirrors. On large machines, blind spots can extend dozens of metres.\n- Control Measures:\n  * Physical Segregation: Hard barricading (fencing, concrete jersey barriers) separating pedestrian pathways from plant operating zones.\n  * High-Visibility Clothing: Mandatory at all times.\n  * Spotters / Dogmen: Dedicated personnel using clear hand signals and radios to guide plant movement.\n  * Positive Communication: Pedestrians must establish direct visual or radio contact with the operator and receive clear confirmation before crossing plant operating zones."
    },
    {
        title: "Module 4: Construction Hazards (Asbestos, Silica, Electrical, Manual Handling)",
        durationMinutes: 45,
        sequenceOrder: 4,
        contentType: 'TEXT',
        contentUrl: null,
        content: "COMMON CONSTRUCTION HAZARDS: ASBESTOS, SILICA, NOISE, UV & ELECTRICAL\n\n1. Asbestos Awareness & Health Risks\n- Nature: Asbestos is a naturally occurring fibrous silicate mineral widely used in Australian building products prior to 1990 (fibro sheets, corrugated roofing, thermal insulation, vinyl floor tiles). Completely banned in Australia since 31 December 2003.\n- Health Consequences: Inhalation of microscopic airborne asbestos fibres causes incurable fatal lung diseases, including Mesothelioma (aggressive cancer of the lung lining), Asbestosis (fibrotic scarring of lung tissue), and Lung Cancer.\n- Discovery Protocol: If you suspect material contains asbestos during renovation, demolition, or excavation: STOP WORK IMMEDIATELY, evacuate and barricade the area, warn coworkers, and report to your supervisor. Asbestos must be tested and removed exclusively by licensed asbestos removalists.\n\n2. Respirable Crystalline Silica (RCS)\n- Generated by cutting, grinding, sawing, or drilling stone, concrete, brick, mortar, or engineered stone.\n- RCS particles penetrate deep into the alveolar regions of the lungs, causing Silicosis (irreversible lung scarring), Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD), and lung cancer. Australia has banned engineered stone due to extreme silica risks.\n- Mandatory Controls: Wet cutting methods, on-tool HEPA dust extraction, and fitted P2/P3 particulate respirators.\n\n3. Noise-Induced Hearing Loss (NIHL)\n- Construction noise from saws, grinders, pile drivers, and mobile plant frequently exceeds safe exposure limits.\n- Action Level: 85 dB(A) averaged over an 8-hour shift, or 140 dB(C) peak noise.\n- Excessive noise damages delicate hair cells in the cochlea, causing permanent, irreversible Noise-Induced Hearing Loss and Tinnitus (ringing in the ears). Hearing protection (earmuffs/earplugs) must be worn in designated hearing protection zones.\n\n4. UV Radiation & Sun Protection\n- Outdoor construction workers have high skin cancer risks (melanoma and carcinomas).\n- Controls: Wear broad-brim hard hat attachments, long-sleeved UPF 50+ clothing, apply broad-spectrum SPF 30+ or 50+ sunscreen every 2 hours, wear UV-rated safety sunglasses, and take breaks in shaded areas.\n\n5. Manual Handling Hazards\n- The most common cause of injury in the construction industry, resulting in Musculoskeletal Disorders (MSDs) such as back strains, ligament tears, and joint damage.\n- Prevention: Use mechanical lifting aids (cranes, trolleys, forklifts), team lifting, plan the route, bend knees, keep loads close to body, and avoid twisting.\n\n6. Electrical Safety & RCDs\n- All electrical installations and tools on construction sites must be protected by Residual Current Devices (RCDs / safety switches).\n- Portable tools and flexible extension leads must be inspected, tested, and tagged by a licensed electrician or competent person every 3 months.\n- 'Look Up and Live': Maintain strict exclusion zones around overhead powerlines (minimum 3 to 8 metres depending on line voltage)."
    }
